 \textbf{Data.} The comma2k19 dataset \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}, released under the MIT licence, contains $33$~hours of commuting on a $20$~km stretch of California's highway 280, recorded with two cars, a Honda Civic and a Toyota RAV4. Both cars log the fixes of a u-blox M8 single-frequency receiver together with its raw measurements, which include the carrier-to-noise density ratio $C/N_0$ of every tracked satellite, the angular rate of a phone-grade gyroscope, and the vehicle speed read from the controller area network (CAN) bus. On the Civic the antenna is inside the car, under the windshield; on the RAV4 it is on the roof, and the received signals are about $15$~dB stronger \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}. For every video frame the dataset provides a reference pose computed offline by an optimiser that fuses the raw GNSS measurements with inertial and visual data; its estimated horizontal error is $0.6$~m per axis for the RAV4 and $1.3$~m for the Civic \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}. The reference is therefore an estimate \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, }\DIFdelend partly computed from the same raw measurements as the fixes\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, and the errors measured against it approximate those of the receiver}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{. The residual is $e_{\mathrm{fix}}-e_{\mathrm{ref}}$, with covariance $P_{\mathrm{fix}}+P_{\mathrm{ref}}-P_{\mathrm{fix,ref}}-P_{\mathrm{fix,ref}}^{\mathsf T}$. It cannot be interpreted as the receiver's absolute error without accounting for these terms. RMSE, NEES and coverage below are relative to this reference, and the nominal chi-square interpretation of the reported NEES is consequently a diagnostic approximation}\DIFaddend . We use seven Civic drives, four for training ($59$~min) and three for testing ($35$~min), and four RAV4 drives ($47$~min) that are never used for training.
 
