-(a) A measurement taken at $t_k-\delta$ but interpreted at $t_k$ reports the range $r(t_k-\delta)=r(t_k)+v\delta$ for a target closing at speed $v$, so the innovation is $\nu_k=v\delta+e_k$ with $e_k\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2)$ and $b=v\delta$: $b=0.30$~m for $\delta=20$~ms and $b=0.75$~m for $\delta=50$~ms. (b) $\mathbb{E}[\nu_k^2/\sigma^2]=(b^2+\sigma^2)/\sigma^2=1+b^2/\sigma^2$, i.e.\ $2.0$ and $7.25$. (c) At $50$~ms the expected NIS exceeds the threshold, while individual measurements are rejected probabilistically; since $\nu_k/\sigma\sim\mathcal{N}(b/\sigma,1)$, the rejection probability is $P(\lvert Z+b/\sigma\rvert>1.96)$, which equals $0.17$ at $20$~ms and $0.70$ at $50$~ms, against $0.05$ without offset. (d) The mean-crossing criterion is $1+b^2/\sigma^2>1.571$, i.e.\ $\lvert b\rvert>0.3\sqrt{0.571}=0.227$~m, giving mean-crossing offsets of $15.1$~ms at $15$~m/s and $113.3$~ms at $2$~m/s. For an actual detection-power criterion, $\sum_{k=1}^{20}\nu_k^2/\sigma^2\sim\chi'^2_{20}(20(v\delta/\sigma)^2)$. Solving $P(\chi'^2_{20}(\lambda)>\chi^2_{20,0.95})=0.95$ gives $\lambda=30.72$, hence offsets $24.8$ and $185.9$~ms. The same nominal false-alarm probability thus supports different detection limits depending on the required power. (e) The same offset is both most harmful and most detectable at motorway speeds; in slow urban traffic a $100$~ms offset is less detectable by this window ($0.2$~m bias), although whether that error is tolerable depends on the application. Since $\partial h/\partial\delta=v$, nonzero relative speed gives sensitivity to the offset. If range or an additional constant range bias is unknown, a constant-speed offset is confounded with that bias; a reference or varying velocity is needed to identify them separately. With sufficient excitation it can be appended to the state vector and estimated (augmented-state or unified spatio-temporal calibration), or removed at the source by hardware time-stamping. The script \repopath{ch08\_\allowbr