+The data of Example~\ref{ex:hybrid-hetero-kf} were generated from a known model, their errors were independent from one sample to the next, and the uncertainty head had the same form as the true noise law. Logged data offer none of these conveniences. This subsection repeats the exercise on real drives from a public dataset, following the steps a practitioner would take: align the data, build a classical baseline, learn a noise model from the residuals, put it into the filter, and check what happens on a different car. Every number below is printed by the script in the folder \codeentry{E4-GNSS} of \codeentry{Examples}, the accompanying downloader retrieves the data (about $110$~MB).
 
 \textbf{Data.} The comma2k19 dataset \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}, released under the MIT licence, contains $33$~hours of commuting on a $20$~km stretch of California's highway 280, recorded with two cars, a Honda Civic and a Toyota RAV4. Both cars log the fixes of a u-blox M8 single-frequency receiver together with its raw measurements, which include the carrier-to-noise density ratio $C/N_0$ of every tracked satellite, the angular rate of a phone-grade gyroscope, and the vehicle speed read from the controller area network (CAN) bus. On the Civic the antenna is inside the car, under the windshield; on the RAV4 it is on the roof, and the received signals are about $15$~dB stronger \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}. For every video frame the dataset provides a reference pose computed offline by an optimiser that fuses the raw GNSS measurements with inertial and visual data; its estimated horizontal error is $0.6$~m per axis for the RAV4 and $1.3$~m for the Civic \cite{schafer2018_comma2k19}. The reference is therefore an estimate partly computed from the same raw measurements as the fixes. The residual is $e_{\mathrm{fix}}-e_{\mathrm{ref}}$, with covariance $P_{\mathrm{fix}}+P_{\mathrm{ref}}-P_{\mathrm{fix,ref}}-P_{\mathrm{fix,ref}}^{\mathsf T}$. It cannot be interpreted as the receiver's absolute error without accounting for these terms. RMSE, NEES and coverage below are relative to this reference, and the nominal chi-square interpretation of the reported NEES is consequently a diagnostic approximation. We use seven Civic drives, four for training ($59$~min) and three for testing ($35$~min), and four RAV4 drives ($47$~min) that are never used for training.
 
